# SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD
"""lib/rtl/mimic_shutil.pas against CPython's real shutil.get_terminal_size.

A DIFFERENTIAL: this file runs unmodified under CPython, where `import shutil`
finds the stdlib module, and produces identical output both ways. Run it as
`python3 test/lib_mimic_shutil.npy` to see that.

WHY THIS IS DETERMINISTIC, which a terminal-size query has no right to be.
Both implementations resolve each dimension the same way: the environment
first, then the real terminal, then the caller's fallback. Under the harness
stdout is a pipe, so the terminal query fails on both sides and the fallback
is what comes out -- the same fallback, because the caller supplies it. If the
harness ever runs this on a tty the first two rows change together and the row
below them does not, which is why the fallback row is the one that asserts.

THE FALLBACK IS 133x47 AND NOT 80x24 ON PURPOSE. 80x24 is also CPython's
DEFAULT fallback and the value ansiterm's TerminalSize leaves in its
out-parameters when the ioctl fails, so a test asserting 80 cannot tell a
fallback that was honoured from one that was ignored, or from a query that
silently failed -- three different outcomes, one number. 133x47 is reachable
only by actually reading the tuple that was passed in. (CLAUDE.md: "if the
machinery did nothing at all, would this row still pass?")
"""

import shutil

# The caller's own fallback, in values nothing else in the path produces.
size = shutil.get_terminal_size((133, 47))
print("fallback columns", size.columns)
print("fallback lines", size.lines)

# Read twice: the query is not supposed to cache or mutate anything.
again = shutil.get_terminal_size((133, 47))
print("stable", again.columns == size.columns, again.lines == size.lines)

# A different fallback must give a different answer, which is what proves the
# argument is read rather than a constant returned.
other = shutil.get_terminal_size((31, 9))
print("second fallback", other.columns, other.lines)

# Both dimensions are positive whatever path was taken -- the one thing a
# caller may assume on any runtime and any terminal.
print("positive", size.columns > 0, size.lines > 0)
